  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY NIGHT THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 310 AM CST Tue Nov 3 2026\n\nCloud cover is forecast to be slow to clear with continued cyclonic \nflow aloft across the area resulting in wrap around moisture into \nWednesday night. There is some signal for clearing, from south to \nnorth by Thursday, which may work to boost afternoon highs about 5 \ndegrees warmer than Wednesday. That would result in highs ranging \nfrom the low 60s to low 70s across the area by the afternoon.\n\nBy Friday, the mid-level trough that is currently across CA finally \ntraverses across the region, bringing an increase in cloud cover and \na 20-30% chance for more rain, along with another cold front. This \nfront appears to actually shift low to mid-level winds out of the \nnorth, advecting in some cooler more seasonable air. Analysis of \nLREF IQRs indicates good agreement that temperatures behind this \nfront would be about 10 degrees cooler. This would bring areawide \nhighs into the upper 50s/lower 60s, with lows in the lower 40s/upper \n30s. In fact, NBM probabilities for lows <32F reach about 50% for \nvalley locations across the Ozarks later this weekend into next \nweek, indicating the potential for a first freeze may not be too far \naway for some.",
